language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ sudoku: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ The goal of Sudoku is to fill a 9x9 grid with numbers so that each row, column and 3x3 section contain all the digits between 1 and 9. The input is a grid with some cells filled with numbers and some empty.
$
$ Save this file as model.eprime. Download `sudoku.json` and `dcp_convert_essence.py` from the
$ problem's page:
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/sudoku.html
$ Convert the instances once, then run the first one with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the
$ solution is written to `sudoku_1.param.solution`:
$   python dcp_convert_essence.py sudoku.json
$   savilerow model.eprime sudoku_1.param -run-solver

$ input_grid[r, c]: the number given in row r, column c (indices 1-based); 0 marks an empty cell
given input_grid : matrix indexed by [int(1..9), int(1..9)] of int(0..9)

$ grid[r, c]: the digit in row r, column c of the solved grid (indices 1-based)
find grid : matrix indexed by [int(1..9), int(1..9)] of int(1..9)

branching on [grid]
such that
$ every filled cell of the input keeps its number
forAll r, c : int(1..9) . input_grid[r, c] = 0 \/ grid[r, c] = input_grid[r, c],
$ each row contains all the digits 1 to 9
forAll r : int(1..9) . allDiff([grid[r, c] | c : int(1..9)]),
$ each column contains all the digits 1 to 9
forAll c : int(1..9) . allDiff([grid[r, c] | r : int(1..9)]),
$ each 3x3 section contains all the digits 1 to 9
forAll b, bb : int(0..2) . allDiff([grid[3 * b + r, 3 * bb + s] | r : int(1..3), s : int(1..3)])
